window.DOCS_DATA=[{"t":"Simple Circuit Examples","items":[{"n":1,"t":"LED in DC source","d":"4.5 V battery -> ammeter -> 330 Ω resistor -> red LED. Forward-biased, so the LED stays ON. The ammeter reads about 8.2 mA and the voltmeter across the LED reads about 1.8 V. Flip the LED (or the battery) and it goes OFF.","net":"* LED in DC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LED in DC source\nV1 vsw 0 DC 4.5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nR1 2 a 330\nD1 a 0 RED_LED\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n.model RED_LED D(Is=7.58e-18 N=2)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 4.5 0.1\n.end"},{"n":2,"t":"LED in AC source","d":"4.5 V peak, 50 Hz AC source with an ammeter and a voltmeter across the LED. The LED is forward-biased on the positive half-cycle (ON, about 1.8 V) and reverse-biased on the negative half-cycle (OFF), so it blinks. No Flip is needed.","net":"* LED in AC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LED in AC source\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 4.5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in 2 DC 0\nR1 2 a 330\nD1 a 0 RED_LED\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n.model RED_LED D(Is=7.58e-18 N=2)\n.tran 0.2m 80m\n.end"},{"n":3,"t":"Bulb in DC source","d":"4.5 V battery -> ammeter -> 30 Ω bulb (4.5 V, 0.15 A, about 0.68 W). Steady DC voltage, so the bulb glows at constant full brightness; the ammeter reads 0.15 A and the voltmeter 4.5 V.","net":"* Bulb in DC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bulb in DC source\nV1 vsw 0 DC 4.5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRBULB1 2 0 30\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 4.5 0.5\n.end"},{"n":4,"t":"Bulb in AC source","d":"4.5 V peak, 50 Hz AC -> ammeter -> 30 Ω bulb, voltmeter across the bulb. Brightness follows v², so the bulb pulses on both half-cycles (twice per cycle) while the meters swing positive and negative.","net":"* Bulb in AC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bulb in AC source\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 4.5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in 2 DC 0\nRBULB1 2 0 30\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.tran 0.2m 80m\n.end"},{"n":5,"t":"Fan in DC source","d":"4.5 V battery -> ammeter -> 120 Ω fan winding. Constant DC voltage, so the fan spins at a constant speed, clockwise; the ammeter reads about 37.5 mA and the voltmeter 4.5 V. Flip the battery (or the fan): the polarity reverses and the fan spins anticlockwise.","net":"* Fan in DC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fan in DC source\nV1 vsw 0 DC 4.5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRFAN1 2 0 120\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 4.5 0.5\n.end"},{"n":6,"t":"Fan in AC source","d":"4.5 V peak, 50 Hz AC -> ammeter -> 120 Ω fan winding, voltmeter across the fan. This is a DC fan, so it follows the changing polarity: clockwise on the positive half-cycle, anticlockwise on the negative half-cycle, speed rising and falling with the voltage. Flipped or not, it moves in both directions.","net":"* Fan in AC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fan in AC source\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 4.5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in 2 DC 0\nRFAN1 2 0 120\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.tran 0.2m 80m\n.end"},{"n":7,"t":"Fan, LED, Bulb in DC source","d":"4.5 V DC battery feeds a 30 Ω Bulb, a 120 Ω Fan and an LED (with a 330 Ω resistor) in parallel. The fan spins clockwise (flip the battery and it spins anticlockwise). The voltmeter reads the 4.5 V supply and the ammeter the total current (about 0.2 A: bulb 0.15 A, fan 0.04 A, LED 8 mA). All three stay on steadily.","net":"* Fan, LED, Bulb in DC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fan, LED, Bulb in DC source\nV1 vsw 0 DC 4.5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nRVM1 1 0 10meg\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRBULB1 2 0 30\nRFAN1 2 0 120\nR1 2 a 330\nD1 a 0 RED_LED\n.model RED_LED D(Is=7.58e-18 N=2)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 4.5 0.5\n.end"},{"n":8,"t":"Fan, LED, Bulb in AC source","d":"4.5 V peak, 50 Hz AC feeds a 30 Ω Bulb, a 120 Ω Fan and an LED (with a 330 Ω resistor) in parallel. The voltmeter reads the supply and the ammeter the total current. The Bulb pulses with |v|; the Fan runs both ways (clockwise / anticlockwise, it is a DC fan); the LED blinks, ON only on the positive half-cycle.","net":"* Fan, LED, Bulb in AC source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fan, LED, Bulb in AC source\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 4.5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nRVM1 in 0 10meg\nVAM1 in 2 DC 0\nRBULB1 2 0 30\nRFAN1 2 0 120\nR1 2 a 330\nD1 a 0 RED_LED\n.model RED_LED D(Is=7.58e-18 N=2)\n.tran 0.2m 80m\n.end"}]},{"t":"DC Resistor Circuits","items":[{"n":9,"t":"Voltage divider","d":"Plot `v(out)` in the DC tab: it is exactly half the input.","net":"* Voltage divider\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltage divider\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nR2 out 0 1k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 10 1\n.end"},{"n":10,"t":"Series resistors","d":"Current is identical through all three; check `i(R1)`, `i(R2)`, `i(R3)`.","net":"* Series resistors\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series resistors\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nR1 1 2 1k\nR2 2 3 2.2k\nR3 3 0 4.7k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 1\n.end"},{"n":11,"t":"Parallel resistors","d":"Branch currents add up to the source current.","net":"* Three parallel branches\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Three parallel branches\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in 0 1k\nR2 in 0 2.2k\nR3 in 0 4.7k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 1\n.end"},{"n":12,"t":"Loaded voltage divider","d":"Compare `v(mid)` with and without `RL`.","net":"* Loaded voltage divider\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Loaded voltage divider\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc mid 10k\nR2 mid 0 10k\nR3 mid 0 4.7k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 1\n.end"},{"n":13,"t":"Wheatstone bridge","d":"`R4` is slightly off balance, so the bridge detector `Rg` sees a small voltage.","net":"* Wheatstone bridge\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Wheatstone bridge\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw top 1G\nR1 top a 1k\nR2 top b 1k\nR3 a 0 1k\nR4 b 0 1.1k\nRg a b 10k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 10 1\n.end"},{"n":14,"t":"Superposition with two sources","d":"","net":"* Superposition: two voltage sources\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Superposition: two voltage sources\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nV2 3 0 DC 5\nR1 1 2 1k\nR2 2 0 2k\nR3 3 2 1k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 10 1\n.end"},{"n":15,"t":"Current source feeding a resistor network","d":"","net":"* Current source network\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Current source network\nI1 0 vsw DC 1m\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nR1 1 0 4.7k\nR2 1 2 1k\nR3 2 0 2.2k\n.op\n.dc I1 0 2m 0.1m\n.end"},{"n":16,"t":"R-2R ladder DAC (3 bit, code 101)","d":"Expected output: 5 V × 5/8 = 3.125 V at `n2`.","net":"* R-2R ladder DAC, code 101\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title R-2R ladder DAC, code 101\nVb2 vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw b2 1G\nVb1 b1 0 DC 0\nVb0 b0 0 DC 5\nR2b b2 n2 20k\nR1b b1 n1 20k\nR0b b0 n0 20k\nRt n0 0 20k\nRa n0 n1 10k\nRb n1 n2 10k\n.op\n.end"},{"n":17,"t":"Delta network with loads","d":"","net":"* Delta resistor network with loads\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Delta resistor network with loads\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nR1 a b 1k\nR2 b c 1k\nR3 c a 1k\nR4 b 0 2.2k\nR5 c 0 2.2k\n.op\n.dc V1 0 10 1\n.end"},{"n":18,"t":"Maximum power transfer","d":"Power in `RL` is highest when `RL = Rs` (see the P column in the operating-point table).","net":"* Maximum power transfer RL = Rs\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Maximum power transfer RL = Rs\nVs vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nR1 1 2 50\nR2 2 0 50\n.op\n.dc Vs 0 10 1\n.end"}]},{"t":"Transient: RC, RL, RLC","items":[{"n":19,"t":"RC charge and discharge (square wave)","d":"Time constant is 1 ms.","net":"* RC charge/discharge\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC charge/discharge\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 20u 10m uic\n.end"},{"n":20,"t":"Slow RC (τ = 1 ms with 10 kΩ and 100 nF)","d":"","net":"* RC time constant 1 ms\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC time constant 1 ms\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 10k\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 20u 10m uic\n.end"},{"n":21,"t":"RL step response","d":"Time constant L/R = 0.1 ms. Plot `i(L1)`.","net":"* RL step response\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RL step response\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 100\nL1 out 0 10m\n.tran 5u 1m\n.end"},{"n":22,"t":"RC differentiator","d":"","net":"* RC differentiator\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC differentiator\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 1m 2m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in out 10n IC=0\nR1 out 0 10k\n.tran 5u 4m uic\n.end"},{"n":23,"t":"RC integrator (square wave to triangle)","d":"","net":"* RC integrator\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC integrator\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(-1 1 0 1n 1n 50u 100u)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 100k\nC1 out 0 10n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 0.5u 1m uic\n.end"},{"n":24,"t":"Series RLC, underdamped","d":"Overshoot and ringing at about 1.6 kHz.","net":"* Series RLC underdamped\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series RLC underdamped\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 1 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 10\nL1 a b 10m\nC1 b 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0\n.tran 5u 2m uic\n.end"},{"n":25,"t":"Series RLC, overdamped","d":"","net":"* Series RLC overdamped\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series RLC overdamped\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 1 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nL1 a b 10m\nC1 b 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0\n.tran 5u 2m uic\n.end"},{"n":26,"t":"Series RLC, critically damped","d":"R = 2·√(L/C) = 200 Ω.","net":"* Series RLC critically damped\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series RLC critically damped\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 1 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 200\nL1 a b 10m\nC1 b 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0\n.tran 5u 2m uic\n.end"},{"n":27,"t":"Parallel RLC ringing (current impulse)","d":"","net":"* Parallel RLC impulse response\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Parallel RLC impulse response\nI1 0 vsw PULSE(0 1m 0 1n 1n 20u 1)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw n 1G\nR1 n 0 100\nL1 n 0 1m\nC1 n 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(n)=0\n.tran 1u 2m uic\n.end"},{"n":28,"t":"Two-stage RC network","d":"","net":"* Two-stage RC low-pass step\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Two-stage RC low-pass step\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5m 10m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nC1 a 0 1u\nR2 a b 1k\nC2 b 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0 v(b)=0\n.tran 20u 10m uic\n.end"},{"n":29,"t":"Capacitor charged through a Thevenin divider","d":"Final value is the divider voltage, not the supply.","net":"* Capacitor charging via divider\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Capacitor charging via divider\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 10k\nR2 a 0 10k\nC1 a 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0\n.tran 50u 50m uic\n.end"},{"n":30,"t":"Charge sharing between two capacitors","d":"","net":"* Coupled RC stages\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Coupled RC stages\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10m 20m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nC1 a 0 1u\nR2 a b 1k\nC2 b 0 2u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0 v(b)=0\n.tran 50u 20m uic\n.end"}]},{"t":"AC Frequency Response and Filters","items":[{"n":31,"t":"RC low-pass (fc ≈ 1.59 kHz)","d":"","net":"* RC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":32,"t":"RC high-pass","d":"","net":"* RC high-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RC high-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in out 100n IC=0\nR1 out 0 1k\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":33,"t":"RL low-pass","d":"fc = R/(2πL) ≈ 1.59 kHz.","net":"* RL low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RL low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nL1 in out 10m\nR1 out 0 100\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":34,"t":"RL high-pass","d":"","net":"* RL high-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title RL high-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 100\nL1 out 0 10m\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":35,"t":"Series RLC band-pass (Q = 10)","d":"Output is taken across `R1`.","net":"* Series RLC band-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series RLC band-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nL1 in a 10m\nC1 a out 1u IC=0\nR1 out 0 10\n.ac dec 100 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":36,"t":"Series LC notch (band-stop)","d":"","net":"* LC notch at 1.59 kHz\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LC notch at 1.59 kHz\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 100\nL1 out m 10m\nC1 m 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(m)=0\n.ac dec 100 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":37,"t":"Parallel LC resonant band-pass","d":"","net":"* Parallel resonant band-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Parallel resonant band-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nL1 out 0 10m\nC1 out 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.ac dec 100 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":38,"t":"Second-order LC low-pass","d":"","net":"* Second-order LC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Second-order LC low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nL1 in out 10m\nC1 out 0 1u\nR1 out 0 50\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.ac dec 50 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":39,"t":"Second-order CL high-pass","d":"","net":"* Second-order high-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Second-order high-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in out 1u IC=0\nL1 out 0 10m\nR1 out 0 100\n.ac dec 50 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":40,"t":"Twin-T notch filter (fn ≈ 1.59 kHz)","d":"","net":"* Twin-T notch\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Twin-T notch\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 10k\nR2 a out 10k\nC3 a 0 20n\nC1 in b 10n IC=0\nC2 b out 10n IC=0\nR3 b 0 5k\nR4 out 0 1meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0\n.ac dec 100 10 100k\n.end"},{"n":41,"t":"Wien bridge network (peak gain 1/3 at 1.59 kHz)","d":"","net":"* Wien network band-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Wien network band-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 10k\nC1 a out 10n IC=0\nR2 out 0 10k\nC2 out 0 10n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.ac dec 100 10 100k\n.end"},{"n":42,"t":"Two-stage RC low-pass (−40 dB/dec)","d":"","net":"* Cascaded RC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Cascaded RC low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nC1 a 0 100n\nR2 a out 1k\nC2 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0 v(out)=0\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":43,"t":"Three-stage RC phase-shift network","d":"The phase plot reaches 180° of lead near 650 Hz (1/(2πRC√6)), where the gain is about 1/29.","net":"* Three-stage RC phase shifter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Three-stage RC phase shifter\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in a 10n IC=0\nR1 a 0 10k\nC2 a b 10n IC=0\nR2 b 0 10k\nC3 b out 10n IC=0\nR3 out 0 10k\n.ac dec 100 10 100k\n.end"},{"n":44,"t":"Lead network","d":"","net":"* Lead network\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Lead network\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 10k\nC1 in out 10n IC=0\nR2 out 0 1k\n.ac dec 50 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":45,"t":"Lag network","d":"","net":"* Lag network\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Lag network\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 10k\nR2 out m 1k\nC1 m 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(m)=0\n.ac dec 50 1 100k\n.end"},{"n":46,"t":"Pi (C-L-C) filter between 50 Ω terminations","d":"","net":"* Pi filter 50 ohm\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Pi filter 50 ohm\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw src 1G\nR1 src in 50\nC1 in 0 1u\nL1 in out 10m\nC2 out 0 1u\nR2 out 0 50\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(in)=0 v(out)=0\n.ac dec 50 100 1meg\n.end"}]},{"t":"Diodes and Rectifiers","items":[{"n":47,"t":"Diode forward I-V curve","d":"","net":"* Diode forward I-V\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode forward I-V\nV1 vsw 0 DC 0\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 100\nD1 a 0 DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 5 0.05\n.end"},{"n":48,"t":"Reverse-biased diode","d":"","net":"* Diode reverse and forward sweep\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode reverse and forward sweep\nV1 vsw 0 DC -5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nD1 a 0 DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.op\n.dc V1 -10 5 0.25\n.end"},{"n":49,"t":"Half-wave rectifier (no filter)","d":"","net":"* Half-wave rectifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Half-wave rectifier\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nD1 in out DX\nR1 out 0 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 0.1m 60m\n.end"},{"n":50,"t":"Half-wave rectifier with filter capacitor","d":"","net":"* Half-wave rectifier with filter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Half-wave rectifier with filter\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nD1 in out DX\nC1 out 0 10u\nR1 out 0 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.end"},{"n":51,"t":"Full-wave bridge rectifier","d":"","net":"* Full-wave bridge rectifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Full-wave bridge rectifier\nV1 a b SIN(0 10 50)\nR1 vsw 0 1meg\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw b 1G\nD1 a p DX\nD2 b p DX\nD3 0 a DX\nD4 0 b DX\nR2 p 0 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 0.1m 60m\n.end"},{"n":52,"t":"Center-tapped full-wave rectifier","d":"","net":"* Center-tapped full-wave rectifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Center-tapped full-wave rectifier\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 10 50)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nV2 b 0 SIN(0 10 50 0 0 180)\nD1 a out DX\nD2 b out DX\nR1 out 0 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 0.1m 60m\n.end"},{"n":53,"t":"Negative clipper (limits at about −0.7 V)","d":"","net":"* Diode clipper\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode clipper\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nD1 0 out DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":54,"t":"Bidirectional clipper (±0.7 V)","d":"","net":"* Bidirectional clipper\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bidirectional clipper\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nD1 out 0 DX\nD2 0 out DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":55,"t":"Biased clipper (limits near 2.7 V)","d":"","net":"* Biased clipper\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Biased clipper\nV1 in 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\nVb vsw 0 DC 2\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw ref 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nD1 out ref DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":56,"t":"Diode clamper (DC restorer)","d":"The signal is shifted upward so its negative peaks are clamped at about −0.7 V; compare `v(in)` with `v(out)`.","net":"* Diode clamper\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode clamper\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 out in 10u IC=0\nD1 0 out DX\nR1 out 0 100k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 5u 10m uic\n.end"},{"n":57,"t":"Voltage doubler","d":"Output approaches about 2·Vpeak minus diode drops.","net":"* Voltage doubler\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltage doubler\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in a 1u IC=0\nD1 0 a DX\nD2 a out DX\nC2 out 0 1u\nR1 out 0 10k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 10u 30m uic\n.end"},{"n":58,"t":"Three series diodes as a voltage reference","d":"Output `v(a)` is about 2 V.","net":"* Series diode reference\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series diode reference\nV1 vsw 0 DC 9\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nD1 a b DX\nD2 b c DX\nD3 c 0 DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 9 0.25\n.end"},{"n":59,"t":"Diode AND gate","d":"","net":"* Diode AND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode AND gate\nVcc vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVa a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 1m 2m)\nVb b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 2m 4m)\nR1 vcc y 1k\nD1 y a DX\nD2 y b DX\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 20u 4m\n.end"},{"n":60,"t":"Diode OR gate","d":"","net":"* Diode OR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Diode OR gate\nVa vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 1m 2m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nVb b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 2m 4m)\nD1 a y DX\nD2 b y DX\nR1 y 0 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.tran 20u 4m\n.end"},{"n":61,"t":"LED with current-limiting resistor","d":"","net":"* LED driver\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LED driver\nVs vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nR1 1 a 330\nD1 a 0 LED\n.model LED D(Is=1e-18 N=2)\n.op\n.dc Vs 0 5 0.1\n.end"},{"n":62,"t":"Low-drop versus standard silicon diode","d":"The low-Is diode (`DS`) is compared with the standard silicon diode (`DX`).","net":"* Low-drop vs silicon\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Low-drop vs silicon\nV1 vsw 0 DC 0\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 100\nD1 a 0 DS\nR2 in b 100\nD2 b 0 DX\n.model DS D(Is=1e-8 N=1.05)\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 5 0.05\n.end"}]},{"t":"BJT Circuits","items":[{"n":63,"t":"Fixed-bias common emitter (supply sweep)","d":"","net":"* NPN fixed bias\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NPN fixed bias\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRb vcc b 220k\nRc vcc c 2.2k\nQ1 c b 0 QN\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vcc 0 12 0.25\n.end"},{"n":64,"t":"BJT output characteristics (Ib = 20 µA)","d":"Plot `i(Vce)` against the swept `Vce`.","net":"* BJT output characteristic\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title BJT output characteristic\nIb 0 b DC 20u\nVce vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw c 1G\nQ1 c b 0 QN\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vce 0 10 0.1\n.end"},{"n":65,"t":"Voltage-divider bias with emitter resistor","d":"","net":"* Voltage-divider bias\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltage-divider bias\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc b 47k\nR2 b 0 10k\nRc vcc c 2.2k\nRe e 0 1k\nQ1 c b e QN\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vcc 0 12 0.25\n.end"},{"n":66,"t":"BJT as a saturated switch","d":"","net":"* BJT switch\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title BJT switch\nVcc vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVin in 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1u 1u 1m 2m)\nRb in b 10k\nRc vcc c 1k\nQ1 c b 0 QN\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.tran 10u 4m\n.end"},{"n":67,"t":"Emitter follower","d":"","net":"* Emitter follower\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Emitter follower\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(6 1 1k)\nQ1 vcc in e QN\nRe e 0 1k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.tran 10u 3m\n.end"},{"n":68,"t":"Common-emitter amplifier: frequency response","d":"","net":"* CE amplifier AC response\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title CE amplifier AC response\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(0 10m 1k) AC 1\nCin in b 10u IC=-0.00012\nR1 vcc b 47k\nR2 b 0 10k\nRc vcc c 2.2k\nRe e 0 1k\nCe e 0 100u\nQ1 c b e QN\nCout c out 10u IC=0.000684\nR3 out 0 10k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(e)=0\n.op\n.ac dec 20 1 1meg\n.end"},{"n":69,"t":"Common-emitter amplifier: transient","d":"","net":"* CE amplifier transient\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title CE amplifier transient\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(0 20m 1k)\nCin in b 10u IC=-0.00012\nR1 vcc b 47k\nR2 b 0 10k\nRc vcc c 2.2k\nRe e 0 1k\nCe e 0 100u\nQ1 c b e QN\nCout c out 10u IC=0.000684\nR3 out 0 10k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(e)=0\n.tran 10u 5m uic\n.end"},{"n":70,"t":"Common-base amplifier","d":"","net":"* Common-base amplifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Common-base amplifier\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(0 20m 1k) AC 1\nCin in e 10u IC=0\nRe e 0 1k\nR1 vcc b 47k\nR2 b 0 10k\nCb b 0 100u\nRc vcc c 2.2k\nQ1 c b e QN\nCout c out 10u IC=0.000684\nR3 out 0 10k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0.00012\n.op\n.ac dec 20 10 1meg\n.tran 10u 3m uic\n.end"},{"n":71,"t":"BJT current mirror","d":"","net":"* BJT current mirror\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title BJT current mirror\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc c1 10k\nQ1 c1 c1 0 QN\nQ2 out c1 0 QN\nR2 vcc out 4.7k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vcc 0 12 0.25\n.end"},{"n":72,"t":"Differential pair","d":"Sweep `Vin1` and watch the collector voltages cross.","net":"* BJT differential pair\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title BJT differential pair\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVee vee 0 DC -12\nVin1 in1 0 DC 0\nVin2 in2 0 DC 0\nRc1 vcc c1 4.7k\nRc2 vcc c2 4.7k\nQ1 c1 in1 e QN\nQ2 c2 in2 e QN\nRe e vee 10k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vin1 -0.2 0.2 0.01\n.end"},{"n":73,"t":"Darlington emitter follower","d":"Emitter voltage is about 1.4 V below the base.","net":"* Darlington pair\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Darlington pair\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVb b 0 DC 5\nQ1 vcc b e1 QN\nQ2 vcc e1 e QN\nRe e 0 1k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vb 0 10 0.1\n.end"},{"n":74,"t":"PNP common-emitter stage","d":"","net":"* PNP common emitter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title PNP common emitter\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRb b 0 220k\nRc c 0 2.2k\nQ1 c b vcc QP\n.model QP PNP(Is=1e-14 Bf=100)\n.op\n.dc Vcc 0 12 0.25\n.end"},{"n":75,"t":"Complementary push-pull follower (crossover distortion)","d":"","net":"* Push-pull emitter follower\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Push-pull emitter follower\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVee vee 0 DC -12\nVin in 0 SIN(0 5 1k)\nQ1 vcc in out QN\nQ2 vee in out QP\nR1 out 0 1k\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model QP PNP(Is=1e-14 Bf=100)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":76,"t":"Constant-current source with diode reference","d":"","net":"* BJT current source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title BJT current source\nVcc vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc b 4.7k\nD1 b m DX\nD2 m 0 DX\nQ1 c b e QN\nRe e 0 470\nR2 vcc c 1k\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vcc 0 12 0.25\n.end"}]},{"t":"MOSFET Circuits","items":[{"n":77,"t":"NMOS transfer characteristic","d":"","net":"* NMOS transfer curve\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS transfer curve\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVg g 0 DC 0\nRd vdd d 1k\nM1 d g 0 0 NM W=20u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.dc Vg 0 5 0.05\n.end"},{"n":78,"t":"NMOS output characteristic (Vgs = 3 V)","d":"","net":"* NMOS output characteristic\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS output characteristic\nVg vsw 0 DC 3\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw g 1G\nVds d 0 DC 5\nM1 d g 0 0 NM W=20u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.dc Vds 0 5 0.05\n.end"},{"n":79,"t":"Common-source stage with gate-voltage sweep","d":"","net":"* NMOS common source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS common source\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVg g 0 DC 2\nRd vdd d 4.7k\nM1 d g 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u)\n.op\n.dc Vg 0 5 0.1\n.end"},{"n":80,"t":"Common-source amplifier: transient","d":"","net":"* NMOS CS amplifier transient\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS CS amplifier transient\nVdd vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin g 0 SIN(2 0.2 1k)\nRd vdd d 4.7k\nM1 d g 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":81,"t":"Common-source amplifier: frequency response","d":"","net":"* NMOS CS amplifier AC\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS CS amplifier AC\nVdd vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(0 10m 1k) AC 1\nCin in g 100n IC=-0.003197\nR1 vdd g 680k\nR2 g 0 320k\nRd vdd d 2.2k\nM1 d g 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nCout d out 100n IC=0.00999\nR3 out 0 100k\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.ac dec 20 10 1meg\n.end"},{"n":82,"t":"Source follower","d":"","net":"* NMOS source follower\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS source follower\nVdd vsw 0 DC 10\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin in 0 SIN(5 0.5 1k)\nM1 vdd in s 0 NM W=20u L=1u\nRs s 0 4.7k\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.tran 5u 3m\n.end"},{"n":83,"t":"NMOS inverter with resistive load","d":"","net":"* NMOS resistive-load inverter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS resistive-load inverter\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin in 0 PULSE(0 5 0 10n 10n 5u 10u)\nRd vdd out 10k\nM1 out in 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nCl out 0 1p fmax=10meg\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=4.99\n.tran 50n 20u uic\n.end"},{"n":84,"t":"CMOS inverter transfer curve","d":"Switching threshold is near 2.5 V because the devices are matched.","net":"* CMOS inverter VTC\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title CMOS inverter VTC\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin in 0 DC 0\nM1 out in 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nM2 out in vdd vdd PM W=20u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-1 Kp=50u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.dc Vin 0 5 0.05\n.end"},{"n":85,"t":"CMOS inverter transient with load capacitor","d":"","net":"* CMOS inverter switching\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title CMOS inverter switching\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVin in 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nM1 out in 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nM2 out in vdd vdd PM W=20u L=1u\nCl out 0 100f fmax=10meg\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-1 Kp=50u Lambda=0.02)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=4.99\n.tran 50n 20u uic\n.end"},{"n":86,"t":"NMOS current mirror","d":"","net":"* NMOS current mirror\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS current mirror\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nR1 vdd g 10k\nM1 g g 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nM2 out g 0 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nR2 vdd out 4.7k\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.dc Vdd 0 5 0.1\n.end"},{"n":87,"t":"NMOS differential pair","d":"","net":"* NMOS differential pair\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS differential pair\nVdd vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nI1 s 0 DC 200u\nVin1 in1 0 DC 2\nVin2 in2 0 DC 2\nRd1 vdd d1 10k\nRd2 vdd d2 10k\nM1 d1 in1 s 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nM2 d2 in2 s 0 NM W=10u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.op\n.dc Vin1 1.5 2.5 0.02\n.end"},{"n":88,"t":"NMOS pass transistor with capacitive load","d":"Output reaches the input voltage while the gate is high, then holds on the capacitor.","net":"* NMOS pass gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NMOS pass gate\nVin vsw 0 DC 3\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVg g 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1u 1u 100u 200u)\nM1 in g out 0 NM W=10u L=1u\nCl out 0 1n\nRl out 0 1meg\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=1 Kp=100u)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 1u 400u uic\n.end"}]},{"t":"Controlled Sources and Op-Amp Circuits","items":[{"n":89,"t":"VCVS amplifier (gain 10)","d":"","net":"* VCVS gain of 10\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title VCVS gain of 10\nVin vsw 0 DC 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nE1 out 0 in 0 10\nRl out 0 1k\n.op\n.dc Vin -1 1 0.1\n.ac dec 10 1 1k\n.end"},{"n":90,"t":"VCCS transconductance stage","d":"","net":"* VCCS transconductance 2 mS\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title VCCS transconductance 2 mS\nVin vsw 0 DC 0\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nG1 out 0 in 0 2m\nRl out 0 2k\n.op\n.dc Vin -1 1 0.1\n.end"},{"n":91,"t":"Inverting amplifier (gain −10)","d":"","net":"* Inverting op-amp\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Inverting op-amp\nVin vsw 0 DC 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in inv 1k\nRf inv out 10k\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.op\n.dc Vin -1 1 0.1\n.ac dec 10 1 1k\n.end"},{"n":92,"t":"Non-inverting amplifier (gain 10)","d":"","net":"* Non-inverting op-amp\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Non-inverting op-amp\nVin vsw 0 DC 0.5 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nRf out inv 9k\nRg inv 0 1k\nE1 out 0 in inv 100000\n.op\n.dc Vin -1 1 0.1\n.end"},{"n":93,"t":"Voltage follower (buffer)","d":"","net":"* Voltage follower\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltage follower\nVin vsw 0 SIN(0 1 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nE1 out 0 in out 100000\nRl out 0 1k\n.tran 10u 3m\n.end"},{"n":94,"t":"Summing amplifier","d":"Expected output: −(1 V + 2 V) = −3 V.","net":"* Summing amplifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Summing amplifier\nV1 vsw 0 DC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nV2 b 0 DC 2\nR1 a inv 10k\nR2 b inv 10k\nRf inv out 10k\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.op\n.end"},{"n":95,"t":"Difference amplifier","d":"Expected output: 100k/10k × (2 V − 1 V) = 10 V.","net":"* Difference amplifier\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Difference amplifier\nV1 vsw 0 DC 2\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nV2 b 0 DC 1\nR1 a inv 10k\nRf inv out 100k\nR3 b np 10k\nR4 np 0 100k\nE1 out 0 np inv 100000\n.op\n.end"},{"n":96,"t":"Op-amp integrator","d":"","net":"* Op-amp integrator\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Op-amp integrator\nVin vsw 0 PULSE(-1 1 0 1n 1n 500u 1m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in inv 10k\nC1 inv out 10n IC=-0.001\nRf inv out 1meg\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.tran 2u 4m uic\n.end"},{"n":97,"t":"Op-amp differentiator","d":"Triangle input gives a square-wave output.","net":"* Op-amp differentiator\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Op-amp differentiator\nVin vsw 0 PWL(0 0 0.5m 1 1m 0 1.5m 1 2m 0)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nRs in x 100\nCin x inv 10n IC=0\nRf inv out 10k\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.tran 5u 2m uic\n.end"},{"n":98,"t":"Active low-pass filter (fc ≈ 1 kHz, gain −1)","d":"","net":"* Active inverting low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Active inverting low-pass\nVin vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in inv 10k\nRf inv out 10k\nCf inv out 15.9n IC=0\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.ac dec 50 10 100k\n.end"},{"n":99,"t":"Active high-pass filter (fc ≈ 1 kHz, gain −1)","d":"","net":"* Active inverting high-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Active inverting high-pass\nVin vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nC1 in a 15.9n IC=0\nR1 a inv 10k\nRf inv out 10k\nE1 out 0 0 inv 100000\n.ac dec 50 10 100k\n.end"},{"n":100,"t":"Sallen-Key low-pass (Butterworth, fc ≈ 1.6 kHz)","d":"","net":"* Sallen-Key low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Sallen-Key low-pass\nVin vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 10k\nR2 a b 10k\nC1 a out 14.1n IC=0\nC2 b 0 7.07n\nE1 out 0 b 0 1\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0\n.ac dec 50 10 100k\n.end"}]},{"t":"Source Waveforms","items":[{"n":101,"t":"Exponentially damped sine","d":"","net":"* Damped sine source\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Damped sine source\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 5 1k 0 500 0)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in 0 1k\n.tran 5u 5m\n.end"},{"n":102,"t":"Square wave through an RC low-pass (harmonic filtering)","d":"","net":"* Square wave into RC filter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Square wave into RC filter\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 0.5m 1m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1.6k\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 5u 5m uic\n.end"},{"n":103,"t":"Arbitrary PWL stimulus","d":"","net":"* PWL source into RC\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title PWL source into RC\nV1 vsw 0 PWL(0 0 1m 0 1.1m 5 3m 5 3.1m 0 5m 0)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 1u\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 10u 6m uic\n.end"},{"n":104,"t":"Two series sine sources (100 Hz beat)","d":"","net":"* Beat between 1 kHz and 1.1 kHz\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Beat between 1 kHz and 1.1 kHz\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 1 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nV2 b a SIN(0 1 1.1k)\nR1 b 0 1k\n.tran 10u 20m\n.end"}]},{"t":"Applications","items":[{"n":105,"t":"Bridge rectifier with reservoir capacitor (ripple)","d":"","net":"* Bridge rectifier with filter cap\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bridge rectifier with filter cap\nV1 a b SIN(0 12 50)\nR1 vsw 0 1meg\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw b 1G\nD1 a p DX\nD2 b p DX\nD3 0 a DX\nD4 0 b DX\nC1 p 0 470u\nR2 p 0 100\n.model DX D(Is=1e-14 N=1)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(p)=0\n.tran 0.1m 100m uic\n.end"},{"n":106,"t":"Series-pass regulator (LED reference)","d":"Output stays near 1.2 V once the input is high enough.","net":"* Series-pass regulator\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Series-pass regulator\nVin vsw 0 DC 15\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vin 1G\nR1 vin b 2.2k\nD1 b 0 LED\nQ1 vin b out QN\nR2 out 0 1k\n.model LED D(Is=1e-18 N=2)\n.model QN NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc Vin 0 15 0.25\n.end"},{"n":107,"t":"Third-order Butterworth LC low-pass (fc = 1 kHz, 50 Ω)","d":"","net":"* 3rd-order Butterworth LC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 3rd-order Butterworth LC low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw src 1G\nR1 src in 50\nC1 in 0 3.18u\nL1 in out 15.9m\nC2 out 0 3.18u\nR2 out 0 50\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(in)=0 v(out)=0\n.ac dec 50 100 100k\n.end"},{"n":108,"t":"LC delay line (4 sections, Z0 ≈ 32 Ω)","d":"The pulse arrives about 32 µs later per section.","net":"* LC delay line\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LC delay line\nV1 vsw 0 PULSE(0 1 0 1u 1u 200u 1m)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw src 1G\nR1 src a 32\nC1 a 0 1u\nL1 a b 1m\nC2 b 0 1u\nL2 b c 1m\nC3 c 0 1u\nL3 c d 1m\nC4 d 0 1u\nR2 d 0 32\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0 v(b)=0 v(c)=0 v(d)=0\n.tran 1u 500u uic\n.end"}]},{"t":"555 Timer Circuits","items":[{"n":109,"t":"555 astable LED blinker (1.4 s)","d":"The built-in 555 model now runs: f ≈ 1.44/((R1+2·R2)·C1) ≈ 0.72 Hz. `IC=0` + `uic` start the capacitor empty. Plot `v(out)`, `v(trig)`, `v(led_anode)`.","net":"* 555 astable LED blinker\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 astable LED blinker\nVCC vcc 0 DC 5\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 100k\nC1 trig 0 10u IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nR_led out led_anode 330\nD_led led_anode 0 RED_LED\n.model RED_LED D(Is=1e-19 N=1.6 Rs=2.5 Cjo=20p)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=3.333\n.tran 5m 6s uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":110,"t":"555 astable 1 kHz clock","d":"f ≈ 1.44/((1k+2·6.8k)·100n) ≈ 986 Hz. `v(trig)` ramps between 1/3 and 2/3 of VCC.","net":"* 555 astable clock ~1 kHz\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 astable clock ~1 kHz\nVCC vcc 0 DC 5\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 6.8k\nC1 trig 0 100n IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=3.333\n.tran 5u 5m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":111,"t":"555 astable with 50 % duty (diode steering)","d":"`D1` bypasses `R2` while charging, so high ≈ 0.69·R1·C and low ≈ 0.69·R2·C.","net":"* 555 astable 50% duty\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 astable 50% duty\nVCC vcc 0 DC 5\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 10k\nD1 disch trig 1N4148\nR2 disch trig 10k\nC1 trig 0 100n IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=3.333\n.tran 5u 5m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":112,"t":"555 monostable one-shot with push button (≈11 ms)","d":"Press the push button (RPB1): its edge, passed through CT, pulls TRIG below 1/3·VCC for a moment and the LED lights for T = 1.1·R1·C1 ≈ 11 ms, then switches itself off. Turn the power switch ON first, then press the button on the canvas.","net":"* 555 monostable with one push button\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 monostable with one push button\nVCC vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nX1 0 trg out vcc ctrl cap cap vcc NE555\nRU vcc btn 10k\nRPB1 btn 0 1G\nCT btn trg 100n IC=0\nRT vcc trg 100k\nR1 vcc cap 100k\nC1 cap 0 100n\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nRL out led 330\nD1 led 0 RED_LED\n.model RED_LED D(Is=1e-19 N=1.6 Rs=2.5 Cjo=20p)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(cap)=0 v(ctrl)=0.00004348\n.tran 50u 30m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":113,"t":"555 Schmitt trigger","d":"TRIG and THRESH tied together: output goes low above 2/3·VCC and high below 1/3·VCC (hysteresis 1.67 V).","net":"* 555 Schmitt trigger\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 Schmitt trigger\nVCC vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVIN in 0 SIN(2.5 2.2 100)\nX1 0 in out vcc ctrl in nc vcc NE555\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(ctrl)=0.00005\n.tran 50u 30m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":114,"t":"555 PWM via control voltage","d":"A slow sine on CTRL (pin 5) moves both thresholds, so the astable duty cycle is modulated.","net":"* 555 PWM using CTRL pin\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 PWM using CTRL pin\nVCC vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVM ctrl 0 SIN(3 0.9 100)\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 10k\nC1 trig 0 10n IC=0\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0\n.tran 2u 20m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":115,"t":"555 bistable with SET and RESET push buttons","d":"Two push buttons: SET (RPB1) pulls TRIG low and the LED turns ON; RESET (RPB2) pulls THRESH high and the LED turns OFF. After you release a button the output remembers the last one pressed. Turn the power switch ON first.","net":"* 555 bistable flip-flop with SET and RESET push buttons\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 bistable flip-flop with SET and RESET push buttons\nVCC vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nX1 0 set q vcc ctrl rst nc vcc NE555\nRS1 vcc set 10k\nRPB1 set 0 1G\nRR1 rst 0 10k\nRPB2 vcc rst 1G\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nRL q led 330\nD1 led 0 RED_LED\n.model RED_LED D(Is=1e-19 N=1.6 Rs=2.5 Cjo=20p)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(ctrl)=0.00005\n.tran 100u 20m uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":116,"t":"555 Amplitude Modulation (AM): 50 kHz carrier, 1 kHz audio","d":"A 555 astable makes a ~50 kHz carrier that switches Q1; the 1 kHz audio rides on the collector supply and an LC tank (≈50 kHz) removes the harmonics, leaving a clean AM envelope. Tick `v(mod_in)` and `v(am_out)`.","net":"* True Amplitude Modulation (AM) using NE555 Timer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n* Architecture: 555 Carrier (~50 kHz) + Transistor Modulator + LC Tank Filter\n.title NE555 Amplitude Modulation (Clean AM Envelope)\n* ==========================================\n* 1. POWER SUPPLY & MODULATING AUDIO SIGNAL\n* ==========================================\nVCC vsw 0 DC 9V\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\n* Modulating Signal: 1 kHz sine wave, 2V amplitude + 3.5V DC offset\n* (Offset keeps the transistor biased cleanly across the entire audio cycle)\nVMOD mod_in 0 SIN(3.5 2.0 1k)\n* ==========================================\n* 2. NE555 CARRIER OSCILLATOR (~50 kHz)\n* ==========================================\n* Pinout: GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl thresh disch vcc NE555\n* Bypass capacitor on Pin 5 for clean carrier stability\nC_CTRL ctrl 0 10n\n* Timing components for ~50 kHz carrier\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch thresh 1.3k\nC1 thresh 0 10n\nV_TRIG thresh trig DC 0\n* ==========================================\n* 3. AMPLITUDE MODULATOR & LC TANK FILTER\n* ==========================================\n* Base drive from 555 carrier switches Q1 ON and OFF at 50 kHz,\n* chopping the 1 kHz modulating signal at the collector.\nR_BASE out base 4.7k\nQ1 col base 0 Q2N2222\n* Collector pull-up to modulating signal\nR_COL mod_in col 1k\n* DC-blocking capacitor into LC Bandpass Tank (resonant at ~50 kHz)\n* f0 = 1 / (2 * pi * sqrt(1mH * 10nF)) = 50.3 kHz\nC_COUP col am_out 10n IC=3.5\nL_TANK am_out 0 1m IC=0\nC_TANK am_out 0 10n\nR_LOAD am_out 0 10k\n* ==========================================\n* 4. MODELS (2N2222 & PURE-ANALOG NE555)\n* ==========================================\n.MODEL Q2N2222 NPN(IS=14.34F BF=255.9 VAF=74.03 IKF=0.2847 ISE=14.34F NE=1.307 BR=6.092 RC=1 CJC=7.306P CJE=22.01P TR=46.91N TF=411.1P)\n.SUBCKT NE555 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nR_DIV1 8 5 5k\nR_DIV2 5 ref_low 5k\nR_DIV3 ref_low 1 5k\nB_COMP_TH comp_r 1 V = { V(6) > V(5) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_COMP_TR comp_s 1 V = { V(2) < V(ref_low) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_NOR1 q_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_r) > 2.5 || V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL1 q_raw q 10\nC_DEL1 q 1 1n IC=0\nB_NOR2 qb_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_s) > 2.5 || V(q) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL2 qb_raw q_bar 10\nC_DEL2 q_bar 1 1n IC=0\nB_OUT 3 1 V = { (V(4) > 0.7 && V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? (V(8) - 1.2) : 0.1 }\nS_DISCH 7 1 q 1 SW_DISCH\n.MODEL SW_DISCH SW(RON=10 ROFF=100MEG VT=2.5 VH=0.1)\n.ENDS NE555\n* ==========================================\n* 5. SIMULATION & PLOTTING COMMANDS\n* ==========================================\n* Initial conditions (.ic): start values for the capacitors; L_TANK starts at 0 A (IC=0)\n* so the tank does not ring at t=0 and the AM envelope is clean from the first cycle\n.ic v(ctrl)=6 v(thresh)=0.08911 v(am_out)=0\n* Run 3ms (3 full cycles of the 1 kHz modulating wave)\n.tran 500n 3m 0 500n uic\n.end"},{"n":117,"t":"555 Pulse Amplitude Modulation (PAM)","d":"A ~20 kHz 555 clock briefly turns Q1 off every ~46 µs so `pam_out` samples the 1 kHz message; pulse heights follow `v(msg)`.","net":"* True Pulse Amplitude Modulation (PAM) using NE555 Timer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n* Architecture: NE555 Narrow-Pulse Sampling Clock (~20 kHz) + Transistor Analog Switch\n.title NE555 Pulse Amplitude Modulation (True PAM)\n* ==========================================\n* 1. POWER SUPPLY & ANALOG MESSAGE SIGNAL\n* ==========================================\nVCC vsw 0 DC 9V\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\n* Message Signal: 1 kHz sine wave, 2V peak amplitude, shifted +3V DC\n* (Positive DC offset ensures PAM pulses stay unipolar 1V to 5V)\nVMSG msg 0 SIN(3.0 2.0 1k)\n* ==========================================\n* 2. NE555 SAMPLING CLOCK (~20 kHz, ~20% Duty Cycle)\n* ==========================================\n* Pinout: GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nX1 0 trig clk_out vcc ctrl thresh disch vcc NE555\n* Control pin bypass capacitor\nC_CTRL ctrl 0 10n\n* Timing components: R1 = 4.7k, R2 = 1k, C1 = 10n\n* Note: In standard astable mode, OUT is HIGH for t_H = 0.693*(R1+R2)*C1\n* and LOW for t_L = 0.693*R2*C1 (~7 us narrow LOW pulse every ~46 us)\nR1 vcc disch 4.7k\nR2 disch thresh 1k\nC1 thresh 0 10n\nV_TRIG thresh trig DC 0\n* ==========================================\n* 3. PAM SAMPLING SWITCH (SHUNT CHOPPER)\n* ==========================================\n* When 555 clk_out is HIGH (~7.8V), Q1 turns ON and clamps pam_out to ~0V.\n* When 555 clk_out goes LOW (~0.1V) for ~7us, Q1 turns OFF and pam_out\n* jumps directly to the instantaneous voltage of v(msg)!\nR_SERIES msg pam_out 1k\nQ1 pam_out base 0 Q2N2222\nR_BASE clk_out base 4.7k\nR_LOAD pam_out 0 100k\n* ==========================================\n* 4. MODELS (2N2222 & PURE-ANALOG NE555)\n* ==========================================\n.MODEL Q2N2222 NPN(IS=14.34F BF=255.9 VAF=74.03 IKF=0.2847 ISE=14.34F NE=1.307 BR=6.092 RC=1 CJC=7.306P CJE=22.01P TR=46.91N TF=411.1P)\n.SUBCKT NE555 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nR_DIV1 8 5 5k\nR_DIV2 5 ref_low 5k\nR_DIV3 ref_low 1 5k\nB_COMP_TH comp_r 1 V = { V(6) > V(5) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_COMP_TR comp_s 1 V = { V(2) < V(ref_low) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_NOR1 q_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_r) > 2.5 || V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL1 q_raw q 10\nC_DEL1 q 1 1n IC=0\nB_NOR2 qb_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_s) > 2.5 || V(q) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL2 qb_raw q_bar 10\nC_DEL2 q_bar 1 1n IC=0\nB_OUT 3 1 V = { (V(4) > 0.7 && V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? (V(8) - 1.2) : 0.1 }\nS_DISCH 7 1 q 1 SW_DISCH\n.MODEL SW_DISCH SW(RON=10 ROFF=100MEG VT=2.5 VH=0.1)\n.ENDS NE555\n* ==========================================\n* 5. SIMULATION & PLOTTING COMMANDS\n* ==========================================\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(ctrl)=0.00002151 v(thresh)=0\n.tran 500n 2m 0 500n uic\n.end"},{"n":118,"t":"555 Pulse Width Modulation (PWM): monostable + pin 5","d":"A fixed 20 kHz trigger starts a one-shot whose width is set by the 1 kHz signal on CTRL (pin 5). Frequency is constant, duty cycle follows `v(ctrl)`.","net":"* True Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) using NE555 Timer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n* Architecture: Monostable One-Shot triggered by Fixed Clock + Analog Signal on Pin 5\n.title NE555 True Pulse Width Modulation (Constant Frequency PWM)\n* ==========================================\n* 1. POWER SUPPLY, CLOCK & MODULATING SIGNAL\n* ==========================================\nVCC vsw 0 DC 9V\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\n* Fixed-Frequency Sampling Clock: 20 kHz (Period = 50 us)\n* Short active-LOW trigger pulses (9V -> 0V for 2 us every 50 us)\nVCLK trig 0 PULSE(9 0 1u 50n 50n 2u 50u)\n* Modulating Analog Signal: 1 kHz sine wave centered at 5.5V, +/- 2.5V swing\n* (Swings Pin 5 between 3.0V and 8.0V to modulate pulse width from ~8% to ~45%)\nVMSG ctrl 0 SIN(5.5 2.5 1k)\n* ==========================================\n* 2. NE555 IN MONOSTABLE PWM MODE\n* ==========================================\n* Pinout: GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nX1 0 trig pwm_out vcc ctrl thresh disch vcc NE555\n* Timing Resistor and Capacitor (Pulse width 4 us to 23 us at 20 kHz)\nR_T vcc thresh 2.2k\nC_T thresh 0 4.7n\n* Connect Discharge (Pin 7) to Threshold (Pin 6) for monostable timing\nV_DISCH disch thresh DC 0\n* Load resistor\nR_LOAD pwm_out 0 10k\n* ==========================================\n* 3. PURE-ANALOG NE555 SUBCIRCUIT MODEL\n* ==========================================\n.SUBCKT NE555 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nR_DIV1 8 5 5k\nR_DIV2 5 ref_low 5k\nR_DIV3 ref_low 1 5k\nB_COMP_TH comp_r 1 V = { V(6) > V(5) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_COMP_TR comp_s 1 V = { V(2) < V(ref_low) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_NOR1 q_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_r) > 2.5 || V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL1 q_raw q 10\nC_DEL1 q 1 1n IC=0\nB_NOR2 qb_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_s) > 2.5 || V(q) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL2 qb_raw q_bar 10\nC_DEL2 q_bar 1 1n IC=0\nB_OUT 3 1 V = { (V(4) > 0.7 && V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? (V(8) - 1.2) : 0.1 }\nS_DISCH 7 1 q 1 SW_DISCH\n.MODEL SW_DISCH SW(RON=10 ROFF=100MEG VT=2.5 VH=0.1)\n.ENDS NE555\n* ==========================================\n* 4. SIMULATION & PLOTTING COMMANDS\n* ==========================================\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(thresh)=0.007628\n.tran 500n 2m 0 500n uic\n.end"},{"n":119,"t":"555 Pulse Position Modulation (PPM): dual 555","d":"Stage 1 makes PWM, a differentiator turns each falling edge into a trigger, and stage 2 emits fixed ~5 µs pulses whose position follows the signal.","net":"* True Pulse Position Modulation (PPM) using Dual NE555 Timers\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n* Architecture: Stage 1 (Monostable PWM) -> Falling-Edge Differentiator -> Stage 2 (Monostable PPM)\n.title NE555 True Pulse Position Modulation (PPM)\n* ==========================================\n* 1. POWER SUPPLY, CLOCK & MODULATING SIGNAL\n* ==========================================\nVCC vsw 0 DC 9V\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\n* Fixed-Frequency Sampling Clock: 20 kHz (Period = 50 us)\nVCLK trig1 0 PULSE(9 0 1u 50n 50n 2u 50u)\n* Modulating Analog Signal: 1 kHz sine wave centered at 5.5V, +/- 2.5V swing\nVMSG ctrl1 0 SIN(5.5 2.5 1k)\n* ==========================================\n* 2. STAGE 1: NE555 PWM GENERATOR\n* ==========================================\n* Pinout: GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nX1 0 trig1 pwm_out vcc ctrl1 thresh1 disch1 vcc NE555\nR_T1 vcc thresh1 2.2k\nC_T1 thresh1 0 4.7n\nV_DISCH1 disch1 thresh1 DC 0\n* ==========================================\n* 3. FALLING-EDGE DIFFERENTIATOR & CLAMP\n* ==========================================\n* Differentiates pwm_out so its falling edge creates a negative trigger spike\n* below 1/3 VCC (3V) for Stage 2, while D_CLAMP clips positive spikes at VCC.\nC_DIFF pwm_out trig2 470p IC=-1.081\nR_DIFF vcc trig2 4.7k\nD_CLAMP trig2 vcc D1N4148\n* ==========================================\n* 4. STAGE 2: NE555 FIXED-WIDTH PPM OUTPUT\n* ==========================================\nX2 0 trig2 ppm_out vcc ctrl2 thresh2 disch2 vcc NE555\n* Bypass Pin 5 on Stage 2 for fixed ~5 us output pulse width\n* t_pulse = 1.1 * R_T2 * C_T2 = 1.1 * 4.7k * 1n = 5.17 us\nC_CTRL2 ctrl2 0 10n\nR_T2 vcc thresh2 4.7k\nC_T2 thresh2 0 1n\nV_DISCH2 disch2 thresh2 DC 0\nR_LOAD ppm_out 0 10k\n* ==========================================\n* 5. MODELS (1N4148 & PURE-ANALOG NE555)\n* ==========================================\n.MODEL D1N4148 D(IS=2.52N RS=0.568 N=1.752 BV=100 IBV=100U CJO=4P TT=20N)\n.SUBCKT NE555 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nR_DIV1 8 5 5k\nR_DIV2 5 ref_low 5k\nR_DIV3 ref_low 1 5k\nB_COMP_TH comp_r 1 V = { V(6) > V(5) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_COMP_TR comp_s 1 V = { V(2) < V(ref_low) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_NOR1 q_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_r) > 2.5 || V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL1 q_raw q 10\nC_DEL1 q 1 1n IC=0\nB_NOR2 qb_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_s) > 2.5 || V(q) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL2 qb_raw q_bar 10\nC_DEL2 q_bar 1 1n IC=0\nB_OUT 3 1 V = { (V(4) > 0.7 && V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? (V(8) - 1.2) : 0.1 }\nS_DISCH 7 1 q 1 SW_DISCH\n.MODEL SW_DISCH SW(RON=10 ROFF=100MEG VT=2.5 VH=0.1)\n.ENDS NE555\n* ==========================================\n* 6. SIMULATION & PLOTTING COMMANDS\n* ==========================================\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(thresh1)=0.005344 v(ctrl2)=0.7873 v(thresh2)=0.002507\n.tran 200n 2m 0 200n uic\n.end"},{"n":120,"t":"555 Frequency Modulation (FM): pin-5 VCO + LC filter","d":"The audio signal drives CTRL (pin 5) so the 555 frequency sweeps around ~25 kHz; Q1 buffers it and an LC filter gives a smooth FM sine at `fm_out`.","net":"* Frequency Modulation (FM) using NE555 Timer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n* Architecture: 555 Pin-5 VCO + Q2N2222 Output Buffer + LC Sine Filter (0V DC)\n.title NE555 Frequency Modulation (Clean Sine FM)\n* ==========================================\n* 1. POWER SUPPLY & MODULATING AUDIO SIGNAL\n* ==========================================\nVCC vsw 0 DC 9V\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\n* Modulating Signal: 1 kHz sine wave, 2.2V amplitude + 6.0V DC offset (natural 2/3 VCC)\n* -90 deg phase starts at trough -> peak -> trough for Low -> High -> Low frequency\nVMOD mod_in 0 SIN(6.0 2.2 1k 0 0 -90)\n* ==========================================\n* 2. NE555 FREQUENCY MODULATED OSCILLATOR\n* ==========================================\n* Pinout: GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl thresh disch vcc NE555\n* Feed modulating signal directly into Pin 5 (Control Voltage)\nR_MOD mod_in ctrl 100\nC_CTRL ctrl 0 1n\n* Timing components (sweeps ~12 kHz to ~48 kHz around ~25 kHz center)\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch thresh 2.2k\nC1 thresh 0 10n\nV_TRIG thresh trig DC 0\n.ic v(thresh)=0\n* ==========================================\n* 3. Q2N2222 BUFFER & LC SINE WAVE FILTER\n* ==========================================\n* Q1 buffers the FM square wave from the 555 output\nR_BASE out base 4.7k\nQ1 col base 0 Q2N2222\nR_COL vcc col 1k\n* DC-blocking capacitor + Damped LC Low-Pass Filter (fc ≈ 55 kHz)\n* Converts FM square wave into a 0V-DC pure sine wave across all frequencies\nC_COUP col filt_in 1u IC=0.622\nR_FILT filt_in n1 1k\nL_FILT n1 fm_out 4.7m\nC_FILT fm_out 0 1.8n\nR_LOAD fm_out 0 1.5k\n* ==========================================\n* 4. MODELS (2N2222 & PURE-ANALOG NE555)\n* ==========================================\n.MODEL Q2N2222 NPN(IS=14.34F BF=255.9 VAF=74.03 IKF=0.2847 ISE=14.34F NE=1.307 BR=6.092 RC=1 CJC=7.306P CJE=22.01P TR=46.91N TF=411.1P)\n.SUBCKT NE555 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nR_DIV1 8 5 5k\nR_DIV2 5 ref_low 5k\nR_DIV3 ref_low 1 5k\nB_COMP_TH comp_r 1 V = { V(6) > V(5) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_COMP_TR comp_s 1 V = { V(2) < V(ref_low) ? 5 : 0 }\nB_NOR1 q_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_r) > 2.5 || V(q_bar) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL1 q_raw q 10\nC_DEL1 q 1 1n IC=0\nB_NOR2 qb_raw 1 V = { (V(comp_s) > 2.5 || V(q) > 2.5) ? 0 : 5 }\nR_DEL2 qb_raw q_bar 10\nC_DEL2 q_bar 1 1n IC=0\nB_OUT 3 1 V = { (V(4) > 0.7 && V(q) > 2.5) ? (V(8) - 1.2) : 0.1 }\nS_DISCH 7 1 q_bar 1 SW_DISCH\n.MODEL SW_DISCH SW(RON=10 ROFF=100MEG VT=2.5 VH=0.1)\n.ENDS NE555\n* ==========================================\n* 5. SIMULATION & PLOTTING COMMANDS\n* ==========================================\n* Run 3ms (3 full cycles of the 1 kHz modulating wave)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(ctrl)=3.701 v(fm_out)=0\n.tran 500n 3m 0 500n uic\n.end"}]},{"t":"CMOS Logic Gates","items":[{"n":121,"t":"CMOS inverter from MOSFETs (transfer curve)","d":"The transistor-level cell behind every gate: PMOS pull-up, NMOS pull-down. Plot `v(out)` against `VIN`.","net":"* CMOS inverter VTC\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title CMOS inverter VTC\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVIN in 0 DC 0\nMP1 out in vdd vdd PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 out in 0 0 NM W=2u L=1u\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.dc VIN 0 5 0.05\n.end"},{"n":122,"t":"NOT gate (CMOS inverter cell)","d":"`y` is the complement of `a`. Cell `INV` comes from the built-in CMOS library.","net":"* NOT gate (CMOS inverter cell)\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NOT gate (CMOS inverter cell)\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nX1 a y vdd 0 INV\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.99\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.end"},{"n":123,"t":"Buffer (two inverters)","d":"`y` follows `a`. Cell `BUF` = INV + INV.","net":"* Buffer (two inverters)\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Buffer (two inverters)\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nX1 a y vdd 0 BUF\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt BUF A Y VDD VSS\nXI1 A m VDD VSS INV\nXI2 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends BUF\n.end"},{"n":124,"t":"AND gate","d":"Inputs A and B count through all four combinations. `y` is high only when both are high.","net":"* AND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title AND gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 AND2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt AND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NAND2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends AND2\n.end"},{"n":125,"t":"NAND gate","d":"NAND2: series NMOS pull-down, parallel PMOS pull-up.","net":"* NAND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NAND gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 NAND2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.99\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.end"},{"n":126,"t":"OR gate","d":"OR2 = NOR2 + inverter.","net":"* OR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title OR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 OR2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x A VDD VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMP2 Y B x VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NOR2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt OR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NOR2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends OR2\n.end"},{"n":127,"t":"NOR gate","d":"NOR2: parallel NMOS pull-down, series PMOS pull-up.","net":"* NOR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NOR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 NOR2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.985\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x A VDD VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMP2 Y B x VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NOR2\n.end"},{"n":128,"t":"XOR gate","d":"XOR2 built from four NAND2 cells: high when inputs differ.","net":"* XOR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title XOR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 XOR2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt XOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B n1 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN2 A n1 n2 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN3 B n1 n3 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN4 n2 n3 Y VDD VSS NAND2\n.ends XOR2\n.end"},{"n":129,"t":"XNOR gate","d":"XNOR2 = XOR2 + inverter: high when inputs match.","net":"* XNOR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title XNOR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b y vdd 0 XNOR2\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.975\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt XOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B n1 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN2 A n1 n2 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN3 B n1 n3 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN4 n2 n3 Y VDD VSS NAND2\n.ends XOR2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt XNOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXX1 A B m VDD VSS XOR2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends XNOR2\n.end"},{"n":130,"t":"3-input NAND gate","d":"A, B and C count in binary; `y` is low only for 111.","net":"* 3-input NAND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 3-input NAND gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nVC c 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 20u 40u)\nX1 a b c y vdd 0 NAND3\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.99\n.tran 40n 40u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP3 Y C VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x1 VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nMN2 x1 B x2 VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nMN3 x2 C VSS VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND3\n.end"},{"n":131,"t":"3-input NOR gate","d":"`y` is high only for 000.","net":"* 3-input NOR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 3-input NOR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nVC c 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 20u 40u)\nX1 a b c y vdd 0 NOR3\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=4.98\n.tran 40n 40u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NOR3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x1 A VDD VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMP2 x2 B x1 VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMP3 Y C x2 VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN3 Y C VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NOR3\n.end"},{"n":132,"t":"3-input AND gate","d":"`y` is high only for 111.","net":"* 3-input AND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 3-input AND gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nVC c 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 20u 40u)\nX1 a b c y vdd 0 AND3\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 40n 40u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP3 Y C VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x1 VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nMN2 x1 B x2 VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nMN3 x2 C VSS VSS NM W=6u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND3\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt AND3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B C m VDD VSS NAND3\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends AND3\n.end"},{"n":133,"t":"3-input OR gate","d":"`y` is low only for 000.","net":"* 3-input OR gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 3-input OR gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nVC c 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 20u 40u)\nX1 a b c y vdd 0 OR3\nCY y 0 100f fmax=10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(y)=0\n.tran 40n 40u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NOR3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x1 A VDD VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMP2 x2 B x1 VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMP3 Y C x2 VDD PM W=12u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN3 Y C VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NOR3\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt OR3 A B C Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B C m VDD VSS NOR3\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends OR3\n.end"},{"n":134,"t":"Half adder (XOR + AND)","d":"`sum` = A ⊕ B and `cout` = A·B, from two library gates.","net":"* Half adder\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Half adder\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nX1 a b sum vdd 0 XOR2\nX2 a b cout vdd 0 AND2\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt XOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B n1 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN2 A n1 n2 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN3 B n1 n3 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN4 n2 n3 Y VDD VSS NAND2\n.ends XOR2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt AND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NAND2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends AND2\n.end"},{"n":135,"t":"Full adder (2 XOR + 2 AND + OR)","d":"Three inputs `a`, `b`, `c`(carry-in); outputs `sum` and `cout`.","net":"* Full adder\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Full adder\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVA a 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)\nVB b 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 10u 20u)\nVC c 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 20u 40u)\nX1 a b s1 vdd 0 XOR2\nX2 s1 c sum vdd 0 XOR2\nX3 a b c1 vdd 0 AND2\nX4 s1 c c2 vdd 0 AND2\nX5 c1 c2 cout vdd 0 OR2\n.tran 40n 40u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt XOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B n1 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN2 A n1 n2 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN3 B n1 n3 VDD VSS NAND2\nXN4 n2 n3 Y VDD VSS NAND2\n.ends XOR2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends INV\n.subckt AND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NAND2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends AND2\n.subckt NOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x A VDD VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMP2 Y B x VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0 fmax=10meg\n.ends NOR2\n.subckt OR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NOR2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends OR2\n.end"},{"n":136,"t":"SR latch from two NOR gates","d":"Cross-coupled NORs remember: `r` clears `q` first, `s` sets it at 3 µs, `r` clears it again at 12 µs.","net":"* NOR SR latch\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title NOR SR latch\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nVS s 0 PWL(0 0 3u 0 3.01u 5 5u 5 5.01u 0)\nVR r 0 PWL(0 5 1u 5 1.01u 0 12u 0 12.01u 5 14u 5 14.01u 0)\nX1 r qb q vdd 0 NOR2\nX2 s q qb vdd 0 NOR2\n.tran 20n 20u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NOR2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 x A VDD VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMP2 Y B x VDD PM W=8u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nMN2 Y B VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0\n.ends NOR2\n.end"},{"n":137,"t":"555 clock gated by an AND gate","d":"A 555 astable (~10 kHz) feeds one AND input; `en` gates it on and off at 200 µs.","net":"* 555 clock + AND gate\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 clock + AND gate\nVDD vsw 0 DC 5\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw vdd 1G\nX1 0 trig clk vdd ctrl trig disch vdd NE555\nR1 vdd disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 6.8k\nC1 trig 0 10n IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nVE en 0 PULSE(0 5 0 1n 1n 200u 400u)\nX2 clk en y vdd 0 AND2\nCY y 0 100f\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=0.000003154 v(y)=0.000001051\n.tran 100n 800u uic\n.model NM NMOS(Vto=0.7 Kp=200u Lambda=0.02)\n.model PM PMOS(Vto=-0.7 Kp=100u Lambda=0.02)\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.subckt NAND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMP2 Y B VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A x VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nMN2 x B VSS VSS NM W=4u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0\n.ends NAND2\n.subckt INV A Y VDD VSS\nMP1 Y A VDD VDD PM W=4u L=1u\nMN1 Y A VSS VSS NM W=2u L=1u\nCL Y VSS 50f IC=0\n.ends INV\n.subckt AND2 A B Y VDD VSS\nXN1 A B m VDD VSS NAND2\nXI1 m Y VDD VSS INV\n.ends AND2\n.end"}]},{"t":"Waveform Sources (Triangle, Sawtooth, Noise)","items":[{"n":138,"t":"Triangle wave through an RC low-pass","d":"`TRI(vo va freq td phase)`: 1 kHz triangle, 1 V amplitude. The RC (fc ≈ 1.6 kHz) rounds the corners; plot `v(in)` and `v(out)` in the Transient tab.","net":"* Triangle into RC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Triangle into RC low-pass\nV1 vsw 0 TRI(0 1 1k) AC 1\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 10u 3m uic\n.end"},{"n":139,"t":"Sawtooth wave into a diode clipper","d":"`SAW(vo va freq td phase)`: ramps from vo-va to vo+va each period. The diode clips the positive ramp near 0.6 V.","net":"* Sawtooth diode clipper\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Sawtooth diode clipper\nV1 vsw 0 SAW(0 2 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nD1 out 0 DX\n.model DX D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 10u 3m\n.end"},{"n":140,"t":"Noise source through an RC low-pass","d":"`NOISE(vo vrms rate seed)`: repeatable gaussian noise, new random value every 1/rate seconds. The RC (fc ≈ 1.6 kHz) removes the fast part of the noise.","net":"* White noise into RC low-pass\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title White noise into RC low-pass\nVN vsw 0 NOISE(0 0.5 200k 1)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 5u 5m uic\n.end"},{"n":141,"t":"Noisy sine cleaned by an RC filter","d":"A 1 kHz sine plus noise (two sources in series). `v(in)` is noisy; `v(out)` after the RC is much cleaner.","net":"* Noisy sine + RC filter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Noisy sine + RC filter\nVS vsw 0 SIN(0 1 1k)\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw a 1G\nVN in a NOISE(0 0.3 200k 3)\nR1 in out 470\nC1 out 0 100n\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 5u 4m uic\n.end"}]},{"t":"Relay Circuits (10 real-life examples)","items":[{"n":142,"t":"Relay changeover: two lamps on one SPDT relay","d":"A 12 V SPDT relay K1 swaps power between two lamps. Relay released: the NC lamp is ON. Relay energised (switch SW1 ON): the contact moves and the NO lamp is ON instead. Click SW1 to toggle. The coil current builds up over a few ms, so the contact moves a moment after the switch (see the Time Domain graph).","net":"* Relay changeover: two lamps on one SPDT relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Relay changeover: two lamps on one SPDT relay\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRSW1 vcc ctl 1m\nXK1 ctl 0 vcc lamp_on lamp_off RELAY_12V\nD1 0 ctl 1N4148\nRBULB1 lamp_on 0 24 Vmax=12 Pmax=7\nRBULB2 lamp_off 0 24 Vmax=12 Pmax=7\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.5m 40m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":143,"t":"Transistor relay driver with flyback diode","d":"A 3.3 V logic pulse cannot drive a 30 mA relay coil directly, so NPN Q1 does it. D1 across the coil absorbs the inductive kick when Q1 turns off (and makes the relay release a few ms later). Plot v(c) and i(XK1.RCOIL).","net":"* Transistor relay driver with flyback diode\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Transistor relay driver with flyback diode\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVIN in 0 PULSE(0 3.3 5m 10u 10u 30m 60m)\nRB1 in b 4.7k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nXK1 vcc c vcc lamp nc RELAY_12V\nD1 c vcc 1N4148\nRBULB1 lamp 0 24 Vmax=12 Pmax=7\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.1m 120m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":144,"t":"Self-latching relay with START / STOP buttons (motor starter)","d":"Classic contactor circuit. A short press of START (S1) energises the DPDT relay; its first contact bridges START (the hold contact) so the coil stays on after the button is released, and its second contact runs the fan. The NC STOP button (S2) breaks the loop at 120 ms and the relay drops out.","net":"* Self-latching relay with START / STOP buttons\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Self-latching relay with START / STOP buttons\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVSTART st 0 PULSE(0 5 10m 100u 100u 20m 1)\nVSTOP sp 0 PULSE(5 0 120m 100u 100u 20m 1)\nS2 vcc a sp 0 PB\nS1 a c st 0 PB\nXK1 c 0 a c nc1 vcc f nc2 RELAY2_12V\nD1 0 c 1N4148\nRFAN1 f 0 120\n.model PB SW(Vt=2.5 Vh=0.5 Ron=0.01 Roff=1G)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.5m 200m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY2_12V CP CN COM1 NO1 NC1 COM2 NO2 NC2\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP1 COM1 NO1 CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON1 COM1 NO1 M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP1 COM1 X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN1 X1 NC1 CP M RLY12_NC\nSNOP2 COM2 NO2 CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON2 COM2 NO2 M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP2 COM2 X2 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN2 X2 NC2 CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY2_12V\n.end"},{"n":145,"t":"Motor reversing with a DPDT relay (fan direction)","d":"Two contacts of a DPDT relay swap the fan polarity. Relay released: the fan runs anticlockwise. Switch SW1 ON: the relay pulls in and the fan runs clockwise. Click SW1 to reverse the motor. D1 protects against the coil kick.","net":"* Motor reversing with a DPDT relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Motor reversing with a DPDT relay\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRSW1 vcc ctl 1m\nXK1 ctl 0 ma vcc 0 mb 0 vcc RELAY2_12V\nD1 0 ctl 1N4148\nRFAN1 ma mb 120\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.5m 40m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY2_12V CP CN COM1 NO1 NC1 COM2 NO2 NC2\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP1 COM1 NO1 CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON1 COM1 NO1 M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP1 COM1 X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN1 X1 NC1 CP M RLY12_NC\nSNOP2 COM2 NO2 CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON2 COM2 NO2 M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP2 COM2 X2 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN2 X2 NC2 CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY2_12V\n.end"},{"n":146,"t":"On-delay timer: RC + transistor + relay","d":"C1 charges slowly through R1. When its voltage is high enough, Q1 conducts and the relay pulls in about 0.7 s after power-on, switching the lamp ON. Change C1 or R1 to change the delay.","net":"* On-delay timer relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title On-delay timer relay\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc tm 100k\nC1 tm 0 10u IC=0\nRB1 tm b 47k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nXK1 vcc c vcc lamp nc RELAY_12V\nD1 c vcc 1N4148\nRBULB1 lamp 0 24 Vmax=12 Pmax=7\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(tm)=0\n.tran 3m 3 uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":147,"t":"Automotive headlight relay (30 mA switch, 10 A lamps)","d":"The dash switch SW1 carries only the coil current (ammeter VAM2, about 30 mA). The relay contacts carry the two 60 W headlamps (ammeter VAM1, about 10 A) straight from the battery, so thin wires and a small switch are enough.","net":"* Automotive headlight relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Automotive headlight relay\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw bat 1G\nRSW1 bat sw 1m\nVAM2 sw ctl DC 0\nD1 0 ctl 1N4148\nXK1 ctl 0 com lamps nc RELAY_12V\nVAM1 bat com DC 0\nRBULB1 lamps 0 2.4 Vmax=12 Pmax=70\nRBULB2 lamps 0 2.4 Vmax=12 Pmax=70\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.5m 40m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":148,"t":"Relay switching a 230 V AC lamp from a 12 V DC control","d":"A small 12 V DC circuit (SW1 + coil) controls a 230 V / 50 Hz lamp through the relay contacts. The contacts only see the AC load; the control side stays low voltage and safe. Click SW1 to switch the lamp.","net":"* Relay switching a 230 V AC lamp from 12 V DC\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Relay switching a 230 V AC lamp from 12 V DC\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vdc 1G\nRSW1 vdc ctl 1m\nD1 0 ctl 1N4148\nVAC ac 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nXK1 ctl 0 ac lamp nc RELAY_12V\nRBULB1 lamp 0 529 Vmax=350 Pmax=250\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 0.12m 120m uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":149,"t":"Over-voltage cut-out relay (zener + transistor)","d":"The supply sweeps 9 V → 18 V → 9 V. Above about 11.7 V the 11 V zener lets Q1 conduct, the relay energises and its NC contact disconnects the lamp. When the voltage falls again the relay releases and the lamp comes back. Plot v(vin), v(lamp).","net":"* Over-voltage cut-out relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Over-voltage cut-out relay\nVIN vsw 0 PWL(0 9 0.3 9 1.2 18 1.8 18 2.7 9 3 9)\nRSWM1 vsw vin 1G\nDZ1 zb vin BZX11\nRB1 zb b 1k\nRB2 b 0 10k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nXK1 vin c vin ok lamp RELAY_12V\nD1 c vin 1N4148\nRBULB1 lamp 0 24 Vmax=12 Pmax=7\n.model BZX11 D(Is=1n N=1 Bv=11 Ibv=1m)\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.tran 5m 3 uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"},{"n":150,"t":"Door / window alarm with a normally-closed loop","d":"Door switch SW1 and window switch SW2 are in series with the relay coil. Door locked (both closed): the relay is held in, the green LED shows \"armed\" and the buzzer beeps ONCE. Click SW1 or SW2 OFF (door open): the relay drops out, the buzzer beeps ONCE again and the red LED BLINKS (555 astable powered by the NC contact) for as long as the door stays open. Each change of state is turned into a single short beep by a capacitor + transistor pulse circuit (Q1 = locked beep, Q2 = open beep). Fail-safe: a cut wire also raises the alarm. Plot v(coil), v(ok), v(alarm), v(bz), v(out).","net":"* Door / window alarm: one beep when opened, one beep when locked, LED blinks while open\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Door / window alarm: one beep when opened, one beep when locked, LED blinks while open\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRSW1 vcc d1 1m\nRSW2 d1 coil 1m\nD1 0 coil 1N4148\nXK1 coil 0 vcc ok alarm RELAY_12V\nRP1 ok 0 10k\nRP2 alarm 0 10k\nR1 ok la 1k\nD2 la 0 GREEN_LED\nC3 ok p1 33u IC=0\nR3 p1 0 22k\nR4 p1 b1 4.7k\nD4 0 p1 1N4148\nQ1 bz b1 0 2N2222\nC4 alarm p2 33u IC=0\nR5 p2 0 22k\nR6 p2 b2 4.7k\nD5 0 p2 1N4148\nQ2 bz b2 0 2N2222\nRBUZ1 vcc bz 400 f=800 Vmax=12 Pmax=2.4\nX1 0 trig out alarm ctrl trig disch alarm NE555\nR7 alarm disch 1k\nR8 disch trig 3.3k\nC1 trig 0 100u IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nRL out lr 680\nD3 lr 0 RED_LED\n.model GREEN_LED D(Is=3.31e-21 N=2)\n.model RED_LED D(Is=1.10e-18 N=2)\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=0\n.tran 5m 3 uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":151,"t":"555 turn-signal flasher with a relay","d":"A 555 astable (about 1.9 Hz) drives Q1, which clicks the relay on and off. The relay contact flashes the indicator lamp, just like a car turn-signal flasher. Plot v(out) and v(lamp).","net":"* 555 turn-signal flasher with a relay\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 turn-signal flasher with a relay\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 3.3k\nC1 trig 0 100u IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nRB1 out b 2.2k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nXK1 vcc c vcc lamp nc RELAY_12V\nD1 c vcc 1N4148\nRBULB1 lamp 0 6.9 Vmax=12 Pmax=25\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.model 1N4148 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=0.000007571\n.tran 5m 3 uic\n.model RLY12_NC SW(Vt=-6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.model RLY12_NO SW(Vt=6 Vh=3 Ron=0.05 Roff=1G)\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.subckt RELAY_12V CP CN COM NO NC\nRCOIL CP M 400\nLCOIL M CN 1.2 IC=0\nSNOP COM NO CP M RLY12_NO\nSNON COM NO M CP RLY12_NO\nSNCP COM X1 M CP RLY12_NC\nSNCN X1 NC CP M RLY12_NC\n.ends RELAY_12V\n.end"}]},{"t":"Transformer Circuits (8 real-life examples)","items":[{"n":152,"t":"Mains step-down transformer: 230 V to 11.5 V lamp","d":"The most common transformer job: a 20:1 transformer brings the 230 V / 50 Hz mains (325 V peak) down to 11.5 V RMS (16 V peak) for a 12 V / 6 W lamp (doorbell, garden light, halogen supply). The secondary is in phase with the primary (dots) and the lamp current is 20 times larger than the primary current. Plot v(ac), v(sec) and i(RBULB1).","net":"* Mains step-down transformer 230 V to 11.5 V\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Mains step-down transformer 230 V to 11.5 V\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 sec 0 XFMR_20TO1\nRBULB1 sec 0 24 Vmax=20 Pmax=15\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.subckt XFMR_20TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS S1 SA 0.0125\nLS SA S2 0.05\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_20TO1\n.end"},{"n":153,"t":"Travel voltage converter: 115 V / 60 Hz to 230 V (step-up 1:2)","d":"A 1:2 step-up transformer lets a 230 V appliance (here a 100 W lamp, 529 ohm) run from 115 V / 60 Hz American mains. Secondary voltage = 2 x primary voltage (163 V peak in, 326 V peak out), while the primary current is twice the load current. Plot v(ac), v(sec), i(RBULB1).","net":"* Travel voltage converter 115 V to 230 V (1:2)\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Travel voltage converter 115 V to 230 V (1:2)\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 163 60)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 sec 0 XFMR_1TO2\nRBULB1 sec 0 529 Vmax=350 Pmax=250\n.tran 0.1m 50m uic\n.subckt XFMR_1TO2 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 2\nRS S1 SA 4.01\nLS SA S2 8\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_1TO2\n.end"},{"n":154,"t":"Isolation transformer 1:1: touching one wire is harmless","d":"A 1:1 isolation transformer (workshop bench, medical equipment) has a floating secondary: there is no return path to earth. SW1 connects a body (1 kohm) to one secondary wire, yet the body current i(RBODY) stays zero, while the 230 V lamp keeps lighting normally. Without the transformer the same touch would send a dangerous current to earth. Click SW1 to connect / disconnect the body.","net":"* Isolation transformer 1:1 (floating secondary)\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Isolation transformer 1:1 (floating secondary)\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 s1 s2 XFMR_1TO1\nRBULB1 s1 s2 529 Vmax=350 Pmax=250\nRSW1 s1 body 1m\nRBODY body 0 1k\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.subckt XFMR_1TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 2\nRS S1 SA 1.01\nLS SA S2 2\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_1TO1\n.end"},{"n":155,"t":"Half-wave rectifier DC adapter (transformer + diode + capacitor)","d":"Cheap wall adapter: a 20:1 transformer gives 11.5 V AC, one diode lets only the positive half-cycles through and the 1000 uF capacitor fills the gaps, so the output is about 14-16 V DC with a 50 Hz ripple (one pulse per cycle, which is why the ripple is large). Plot v(sec), v(out), i(RL1).","net":"* Half-wave rectifier adapter\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Half-wave rectifier adapter\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 sec 0 XFMR_20TO1\nD1 sec out 1N4007\nC1 out 0 1000u IC=0\nRL1 out 0 100\n.model 1N4007 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 0.1m 200m uic\n.subckt XFMR_20TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS S1 SA 0.0125\nLS SA S2 0.05\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_20TO1\n.end"},{"n":156,"t":"Bridge rectifier linear power supply (unregulated DC)","d":"The classic bench supply front end: transformer, four-diode bridge (D1-D4) and a 2200 uF reservoir capacitor. Both half-cycles are used, so the ripple is at 100 Hz and much smaller than in the half-wave circuit. Output = secondary peak (16 V) minus two diode drops, about 14 V DC. Plot v(s1), v(s2), v(out).","net":"* Bridge rectifier power supply\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bridge rectifier power supply\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 s1 s2 XFMR_20TO1\nD1 s1 out 1N4007\nD2 s2 out 1N4007\nD3 0 s1 1N4007\nD4 0 s2 1N4007\nC1 out 0 2200u IC=0\nRL1 out 0 47\n.model 1N4007 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(out)=0\n.tran 0.1m 200m uic\n.subckt XFMR_20TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS S1 SA 0.0125\nLS SA S2 0.05\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_20TO1\n.end"},{"n":157,"t":"Dual +/-15 V supply from a centre-tapped transformer","d":"Op-amp and audio amplifiers need a positive and a negative rail. A centre-tapped 10:1 secondary (each half 11.5 V RMS) has its tap as the 0 V reference; a bridge feeds two reservoir capacitors in series, giving about +15 V and -15 V around the tap. Plot v(s1), v(pos), v(neg).","net":"* Dual +/-15 V supply, centre-tapped transformer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Dual +/-15 V supply, centre-tapped transformer\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 s1 0 s2 XFMR_CT_10TO1\nD1 s1 pos 1N4007\nD2 s2 pos 1N4007\nD3 neg s1 1N4007\nD4 neg s2 1N4007\nC1 pos 0 1000u IC=0\nC2 0 neg 1000u IC=0\nRL1 pos 0 150\nRL2 0 neg 150\n.model 1N4007 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(pos)=0 v(neg)=0\n.tran 0.1m 200m uic\n.subckt XFMR_CT_10TO1 P1 P2 S1 CT S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS1 S1 SA 0.015\nLS1 SA CT 0.05\nRS2 CT SB 0.015\nLS2 SB S2 0.05\nK12 LP LS1 0.995\nK13 LP LS2 0.995\nK23 LS1 LS2 0.995\n.ends XFMR_CT_10TO1\n.end"},{"n":158,"t":"Regulated 5 V supply: transformer + bridge + zener pass transistor","d":"Transformer, bridge and 1000 uF capacitor give about 14 V of rippling DC (v(raw)). A 5.6 V zener fixes the base of the pass transistor Q1, whose emitter follows at one Vbe lower: a steady 4.9 V output (v(reg)) that hardly moves with the ripple. Plot v(raw), v(z), v(reg).","net":"* Regulated 5 V supply\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Regulated 5 V supply\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 s1 s2 XFMR_20TO1\nD1 s1 raw 1N4007\nD2 s2 raw 1N4007\nD3 0 s1 1N4007\nD4 0 s2 1N4007\nC1 raw 0 1000u IC=0\nR1 raw z 330\nDZ1 0 z BZX5V6\nQ1 raw z reg 2N2222\nRL1 reg 0 100\nC2 reg 0 100u IC=0\n.model 1N4007 D(Is=2.52n N=1.75)\n.model BZX5V6 D(Is=1n N=1 Bv=5.6 Ibv=5m)\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(raw)=0 v(reg)=0\n.tran 0.1m 200m uic\n.subckt XFMR_20TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS S1 SA 0.0125\nLS SA S2 0.05\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_20TO1\n.end"},{"n":159,"t":"Microphone input transformer (1:2): low-Z mic to high-Z preamp","d":"A 200 ohm microphone (20 mV peak, 1 kHz) feeds a 1:2 transformer in front of a 10 kohm preamp input. The transformer doubles the voltage (+6 dB, noise-free passive gain) and presents the preamp load to the mic as 10k / 4 = 2.5 kohm, a better match than connecting the mic directly. Plot v(mic), v(ac), v(pre).","net":"* Microphone input transformer 1:2\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Microphone input transformer 1:2\nVMIC vsw 0 SIN(0 20m 1k)\nRSWM1 vsw mic 1G\nRMIC1 mic ac 200\nXT1 ac 0 pre 0 XFMR_1TO2\nRIN1 pre 0 10k\n.tran 20u 5m uic\n.subckt XFMR_1TO2 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 2\nRS S1 SA 4.01\nLS SA S2 8\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_1TO2\n.end"}]},{"t":"Buzzer Circuits (5 real-life examples)","items":[{"n":160,"t":"Doorbell buzzer from a mains transformer (push button)","d":"A 20:1 transformer turns 230 V mains into 11.5 V AC for a 12 V doorbell buzzer. The push button SW1 is ON, so the buzzer sounds a soft 700 Hz tone and its sound waves pulse on the schematic. Click SW1 to release the button (silence). Select the buzzer and change Sound frequency in Properties to hear another pitch. Plot v(ac), v(sec), i(RBUZ1).","net":"* Doorbell buzzer from a mains transformer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Doorbell buzzer from a mains transformer\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 325 50)\nRSWM1 vsw ac 1G\nXT1 ac 0 sec 0 XFMR_20TO1\nRSW1 sec b 1m\nRBUZ1 b 0 100 f=700\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.subckt XFMR_20TO1 P1 P2 S1 S2\nRP P1 PA 1\nLP PA P2 20\nRS S1 SA 0.0125\nLS SA S2 0.05\nK1 LP LS 0.995\n.ends XFMR_20TO1\n.end"},{"n":161,"t":"Push-button buzzer on a 12 V battery","d":"The simplest buzzer circuit: battery, push button SW1 and a 12 V / 30 mA buzzer (400 ohm). With SW1 ON the buzzer sounds at the default comfortable 800 Hz. Click SW1 to switch it off. Plot v(vcc), v(b), i(RBUZ1).","net":"* Push-button buzzer on a 12 V battery\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Push-button buzzer on a 12 V battery\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nRSW1 vcc b 1m\nRBUZ1 b 0 400 f=800\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 0.5\n.end"},{"n":162,"t":"Transistor-driven buzzer: 3.3 V logic beeps a 5 V buzzer","d":"A microcontroller pin (3.3 V pulse, 0.25 s on every 0.5 s) cannot supply the buzzer current, so NPN transistor Q1 switches it from the 5 V rail. The buzzer beeps twice a second at 900 Hz. Plot v(in), v(c), i(RBUZ1).","net":"* Transistor-driven buzzer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Transistor-driven buzzer\nV1 vsw 0 DC 5\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nVIN in 0 PULSE(0 3.3 0.1 1m 1m 0.25 0.5)\nRB1 in b 2.2k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nRBUZ1 vcc c 120 f=900\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.tran 5m 2 uic\n.end"},{"n":163,"t":"555 beeper: smoke-alarm style intermittent beep","d":"A 555 astable (about 1.9 Hz) drives Q1, which turns the 12 V buzzer on and off: a repeating beep-beep at 1000 Hz like a smoke detector or reversing alarm. Change C1 or R2 for a faster or slower beep, or the buzzer frequency for a different pitch. Plot v(out), v(c), i(RBUZ1).","net":"* 555 beeper with buzzer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title 555 beeper with buzzer\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nX1 0 trig out vcc ctrl trig disch vcc NE555\nR1 vcc disch 1k\nR2 disch trig 3.3k\nC1 trig 0 100u IC=0\nC2 ctrl 0 10n\nRB1 out b 2.2k\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nRBUZ1 vcc c 400 f=1000\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(trig)=0 v(ctrl)=0.000007571\n.tran 5m 3 uic\n.subckt NE555 GND TRIG OUT RESET CTRL THRESH DISCH VCC\nR_div1 VCC CTRL 5k\nR_div2 CTRL V_third 5k\nR_div3 V_third GND 5k\nB_comp1 R_int 0 V = (V(THRESH) > V(CTRL)) ? 1 : 0\nB_comp2 S_int 0 V = (V(TRIG) < V(V_third)) ? 1 : 0\nB_latch Q_bar 0 V = (V(RESET) < 0.7) ? 1 : ((V(R_int) > 0.5) ? 1 : ((V(S_int) > 0.5) ? 0 : V(Q_bar_del)))\nR_del Q_bar Q_bar_del 100\nC_del Q_bar_del 0 10p IC=0\nB_out OUT 0 V = (V(Q_bar_del) < 0.5) ? (V(VCC) - 1.2) : 0.1\nS_disch DISCH GND Q_bar_del 0 SW_DISCH\n.model SW_DISCH SW(Ron=10 Roff=10Meg Vt=0.5 Vh=0.05)\n.ends NE555\n.end"},{"n":164,"t":"Burglar alarm: normally-closed door and window loop","d":"Door switch SW1 and window switch SW2 are in series and hold the base of Q1 at 0 V while both are closed: silent. Click SW1 or SW2 OFF (door or window opened) and R1 feeds the base, Q1 conducts and the buzzer sounds. A cut wire raises the alarm too (fail-safe). Plot v(b), v(c), i(RBUZ1).","net":"* Burglar alarm with normally-closed loop\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Burglar alarm with normally-closed loop\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw vcc 1G\nR1 vcc b 4.7k\nRSW1 b w 1m\nRSW2 w 0 1m\nQ1 c b 0 2N2222\nRBUZ1 vcc c 400 f=800\n.model 2N2222 NPN(Is=1e-14 Bf=200)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 0.5\n.end"}]},{"t":"Short Circuit and Burning Situation","items":[{"n":165,"t":"Capacitor Burn or Short Circuit","d":"A 60 V DC battery charges a capacitor through 100 ohm. The capacitor has the default 50 V limit (property Vmax), so when its voltage climbs past 50 V it overheats (smoke), burns (fire, black char) and stays burnt = open circuit. A capacitor also burns when the source frequency is above its Max frequency (default 90 kHz). Fix: lower the battery to 50 V or less, or raise Max voltage in Properties.","net":"* Capacitor burn: 60 V on a 50 V capacitor\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Capacitor burn: 60 V on a 50 V capacitor\nV1 vsw 0 DC 60\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nR1 2 a 100\nC1 a 0 100u IC=0\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(a)=0\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.end"},{"n":166,"t":"Inductor Burn or Short Circuit","d":"A 12 V DC battery drives a 10 mH inductor through only 1 ohm. At DC an inductor has no reactance, so it is a plain wire: the current climbs toward 12 A, far above the 1 A limit, and the inductor burns out (open circuit). Fix: add a larger series resistor, or use an AC source whose reactance is high.","net":"* Inductor burn: DC through an inductor\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Inductor burn: DC through an inductor\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nR1 2 a 1\nL1 a 0 10m IC=0\n.tran 0.1m 60m uic\n.end"},{"n":167,"t":"DC Source Short Circuit","d":"A 12 V battery is connected across a 0.1 ohm resistor, which is almost a dead short. The battery has to deliver 120 A (limit 20 A), so the source overheats, burns and the simulation cannot continue. Fix: use a normal load (bulb, resistor) such as 24 ohm.","net":"* DC source short circuit: 12 V across 0.1 ohm\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title DC source short circuit: 12 V across 0.1 ohm\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nR1 2 0 0.1\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 0.5\n.end"},{"n":168,"t":"Bulb Burn Circuit","d":"A 24 V battery is put across a bulb made for 12 V. The bulb sees 24 V (limit 12 V) and 1 A (limit 200 mA), glows far too bright, heats up, burns out and stays dark. Fix: use a 12 V battery or add a series resistor.","net":"* Bulb burn: 24 V on a 12 V bulb\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Bulb burn: 24 V on a 12 V bulb\nV1 vsw 0 DC 24\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRBULB1 2 0 24\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 24 1\n.end"},{"n":169,"t":"Fan Burn Circuit","d":"A 24 V battery drives a fan winding rated for 12 V. The winding gets 24 V (limit 12 V), spins too fast, overheats, smokes and burns out. A burnt fan is an open circuit and stops. Fix: use a 12 V battery or a series resistor.","net":"* Fan burn: 24 V on a 12 V fan\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fan burn: 24 V on a 12 V fan\nV1 vsw 0 DC 24\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRFAN1 2 0 120\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 24 1\n.end"},{"n":170,"t":"Led Burn Circuit","d":"A 12 V battery drives a red LED through only 100 ohm. The LED current is about 100 mA, five times its 20 mA limit, so the LED flashes bright, overheats and burns out (dark, open circuit). Fix: use a resistor of about 560 ohm or more (R = (12 - 1.8) / 0.018).","net":"* LED burn: no proper current-limiting resistor\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title LED burn: no proper current-limiting resistor\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nR1 2 a 100\nD1 a 0 RED_LED\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n.model RED_LED D(Is=7.58e-18 N=2)\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 0.5\n.end"},{"n":171,"t":"Fuse Burn Circuit","d":"A 12 V battery feeds a 6 ohm load through a 1 A fuse (RFUSE1 ... I=1). The load tries to draw 2 A, so the fuse blows and the circuit opens: the load goes dark. The fuse part stays on the board; press Replace fuse in the Properties panel to reset it. Fix: use a fuse rated above the load current or a bigger load resistance.","net":"* Fuse burn: 2 A load on a 1 A fuse\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Fuse burn: 2 A load on a 1 A fuse\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRFUSE1 2 3 10m I=1\nRBULB1 3 0 6\nRVM1 3 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 12 0.5\n.end"},{"n":172,"t":"Buzzer Burn Circuit","d":"A 24 V battery drives a buzzer made for 12 V (400 ohm, 800 Hz). The buzzer sees 24 V (limit 12 V, same as the fan), so it overheats, smokes, burns out, goes silent and stays an open circuit. Switch ON to hear it for a moment, then it burns. Fix: use a 12 V battery or add a series resistor. Plot v(1), v(2).","net":"* Buzzer burn: 24 V on a 12 V buzzer\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Buzzer burn: 24 V on a 12 V buzzer\nV1 vsw 0 DC 24\n* RSWM1 = power switch: ON = 1m ohm, OFF = 1G ohm (OFF also stops the simulation)\nRSWM1 vsw 1 1G\nVAM1 1 2 DC 0\nRBUZ1 2 0 400 f=800\nRVM1 2 0 10meg\n.op\n.dc V1 0 24 1\n.end"}]},{"t":"Measurement tools in circuits","items":[{"n":173,"t":"Voltage measurement in circuit","d":"A 12 V supply feeds R1 = 1 k, R2 = 2.2 k and R3 = 1 k in series. A voltmeter is always connected IN PARALLEL with the part it measures. DMM1 (dial V=) is across the supply and reads 12 V. DMM2 (dial V=) is across R2 and reads 6.29 V, because the current is 12 V / 4.2 k = 2.86 mA and V(R2) = 2.86 mA x 2.2 k. Turn the power switch ON. The red probe goes to the higher voltage, otherwise the reading is negative. The meter has 10 Mohm input resistance, so it hardly changes the circuit. Plot v(in), v(a), v(b).","net":"* Voltage measurement in circuit\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltage measurement in circuit\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 1k\nR2 a b 2.2k\nR3 b 0 1k\nRDMMDV1 in 0 10meg\nRDMMDV2 a b 10meg\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"},{"n":174,"t":"Current measurement in circuit","d":"An ammeter is connected IN SERIES: the circuit must be opened and the meter put in the gap. DMM1 (dial A=) is in series with the supply and reads the TOTAL current 17.45 mA. DMM2 (dial A=) is in series with the 2.2 k branch only and reads 5.45 mA (12 V / 2.2 k); the 1 k branch takes the other 12 mA. The red probe is on the side where the current comes from. An ammeter has almost no resistance, so never put it across a source (the meter burns at 100 A). Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(in), v(a), i(VDMMDA1), i(VDMMDA2).","net":"* Current measurement in circuit\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Current measurement in circuit\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVDMMDA1 in a DC 0\nR1 a 0 1k\nR2 a c 2.2k\nVDMMDA2 c 0 DC 0\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"},{"n":175,"t":"Resistance measurement in circuit","d":"The circuit has NO supply: a resistance meter (dial Ohm) puts its own 0.4 V test voltage on the part and measures the test current, so R = 0.4 V / I. The switch RSWM1 is the meter power switch: turn it ON to measure. R4 is marked 4.7 k, but the meter reads 2.53 k: in circuit R4 is in parallel with the path R2 + R3 = 5.5 k (R1 hangs on the side and does not count), 4.7 k || 5.5 k = 2.53 k. To read the real 4.7 k, lift one end of R4 out of the circuit (delete the wire to node b). Never measure resistance in a powered circuit. Plot v(b).","net":"* Resistance measurement in circuit\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Resistance measurement in circuit\nVDMMOH1 vsw 0 DC 0.4\nRSWM1 vsw b 1G\nR1 a b 1k\nR2 b c 2.2k\nR3 c 0 3.3k\nR4 b 0 4.7k\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"},{"n":176,"t":"Inductance measurement in circuit","d":"A basic DMM has no inductance range, so L is found with an AC test at 1 kHz (5 V RMS source, 100 ohm protection resistor). DMM1 (dial A~) is in series and reads the current 42.3 mA RMS. DMM2 (dial V~) is across the inductor and reads 2.66 V RMS. The inductive reactance is XL = V / I = 62.9 ohm, so L = XL / (2 x pi x f) = 2.66 / (2 x pi x 1000 x 0.0423) = 10 mH. Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(in), v(b), i(VDMMAA1).","net":"* Inductance measurement in circuit\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Inductance measurement in circuit\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 7.071 1k)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 100\nVDMMAA1 a b DC 0\nL1 b 0 10m\nRDMMAV1 b 0 10meg\n.tran 20u 20m\n.end"},{"n":177,"t":"Capacitance measurement in circuit","d":"A basic DMM has no capacitance range, so C is found with an AC test at 1 kHz (5 V RMS source, 100 ohm protection resistor). DMM1 (dial A~) is in series and reads the current 26.6 mA RMS. DMM2 (dial V~) is across the capacitor and reads 4.23 V RMS. The capacitive reactance is XC = V / I = 159 ohm, so C = I / (2 x pi x f x V) = 0.0266 / (2 x pi x 1000 x 4.23) = 1 uF. Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(in), v(b), i(VDMMAA1).","net":"* Capacitance measurement in circuit\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Capacitance measurement in circuit\nV1 vsw 0 SIN(0 7.071 1k)\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nR1 in a 100\nVDMMAA1 a b DC 0\nC1 b 0 1u IC=0\nRDMMAV1 b 0 10meg\n* Initial conditions (auto): capacitor start voltages, used with uic\n.ic v(b)=0\n.tran 20u 20m uic\n.end"},{"n":178,"t":"Ammeter and voltmeter: Ohm law, R = V / I","d":"The Ammeter (A) is put IN SERIES to measure current and the Voltmeter (V) is put IN PARALLEL to measure voltage. A 10 V supply drives R1 = 470 ohm. The ammeter reads 21.3 mA and the voltmeter reads 10 V, so R = V / I = 10 V / 21.3 mA = 470 ohm (Ohm law). The ammeter has 0 ohm and the voltmeter 10 Mohm, so neither changes the circuit. Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(in), v(a), i(VAM1).","net":"* Ammeter and voltmeter: Ohm law, R = V / I\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Ammeter and voltmeter: Ohm law, R = V / I\nV1 vsw 0 DC 10\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in a DC 0\nR1 a 0 470\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"},{"n":179,"t":"Ammeter in each branch: total current is the sum of branch currents","d":"Kirchhoff current law. A 12 V supply feeds three parallel branches R1 = 1 k, R2 = 2.2 k and R3 = 3.3 k. Ammeters VAM2, VAM3 and VAM4 are in series with the branches and read 12.0 mA, 5.45 mA and 3.64 mA. Ammeter VAM1 is in series with the supply and reads the total 21.1 mA, which is the sum of the three branch currents. The voltmeter RVM1 across the parallel group reads 12 V (every branch has the same voltage). Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(a), i(VAM1), i(VAM2), i(VAM3), i(VAM4).","net":"* Ammeter in each branch: total current is the sum of branch currents\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Ammeter in each branch: total current is the sum of branch currents\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in a DC 0\nVAM2 a b DC 0\nR1 b 0 1k\nVAM3 a c DC 0\nR2 c 0 2.2k\nVAM4 a d DC 0\nR3 d 0 3.3k\nRVM1 a 0 10meg\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"},{"n":180,"t":"Voltmeter across each resistor: voltage drops add up to the supply","d":"Kirchhoff voltage law. A 12 V supply feeds R1 = 1 k, R2 = 2.2 k and R3 = 3.3 k in series. The ammeter VAM1 reads the one series current 1.85 mA. Voltmeters RVM1, RVM2 and RVM3 are across R1, R2 and R3 and read 1.85 V, 4.06 V and 6.09 V. Their sum is 12 V, the same as voltmeter RVM4 across the supply. The biggest resistor takes the biggest voltage drop. Turn the power switch ON. Plot v(in), v(a), v(b), v(c), i(VAM1).","net":"* Voltmeter across each resistor: voltage drops add up to the supply\n* Author Md. Anisur Rahman\n.title Voltmeter across each resistor: voltage drops add up to the supply\nV1 vsw 0 DC 12\nRSWM1 vsw in 1G\nVAM1 in a DC 0\nR1 a b 1k\nR2 b c 2.2k\nR3 c 0 3.3k\nRVM1 a b 10meg\nRVM2 b c 10meg\nRVM3 c 0 10meg\nRVM4 in 0 10meg\n.tran 1m 50m\n.end"}]}];